1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:09,599
Stop!

2
00:01:59,122 --> 00:02:04,000
Ahh... is it quitting time yet...

3
00:02:04,266 --> 00:02:08,000
There are still about 7 hours until quitting time.

4
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:10,600
(Akane) Ugggh...

5
00:02:12,019 --> 00:02:14,352
You seem to be having fun, Lt. Commander.

6
00:02:14,786 --> 00:02:17,452
(Lt. Commander) I'm developing that system! *smugly*

7
00:02:17,869 --> 00:02:19,600
Oh, actual work?

8
00:02:19,723 --> 00:02:20,723
That's rare.

9
00:02:22,266 --> 00:02:23,624
Observe.

10
00:02:23,649 --> 00:02:26,877
I've just implemented a feature that plays sound effects with keyboard input.

11
00:02:28,192 --> 00:02:32,592
(Keyboard sound effects) Beep beep beep beep beep...

12
00:02:32,778 --> 00:02:35,784
(Computer) Katsudon bowl has been ordered.

13
00:02:38,242 --> 00:02:41,772
Plus, sound effects only play when Scroll Lock is ON -- thoughtful design!

14
00:02:42,586 --> 00:02:44,516
Nobody uses Scroll Lock anyway!

15
00:02:48,414 --> 00:02:50,300
Well? Logical, isn't it?

16
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:53,140
The OS I created!

17
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:56,780
Please do some actual work.

18
00:02:58,460 --> 00:02:59,460
Huh?

19
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:00,900
Look who's talking!

20
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:03,523
(Phone ringing)

21
00:03:06,510 --> 00:03:09,110
Hello, this is Hosono from the System Division.

22
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:11,890
(Phone voice) This is Sasayama from the National Railways Police.

23
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:13,583
(Phone voice) Thank you for your continued support.

24
00:03:13,667 --> 00:03:14,699
Huh, an outside call!?

25
00:03:14,730 --> 00:03:16,710
Oh, th-thank you for your continued support.

26
00:03:17,830 --> 00:03:18,830
(Phone voice) ...Huh?

27
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:20,199
(Phone voice) Akane?

28
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:21,866
Huh?

29
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:27,930
I knew it was Akane!

30
00:03:29,048 --> 00:03:31,932
Long time no see! It's Sasamin!

31
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,137
I'm in Himeji today!

32
00:03:35,865 --> 00:03:36,931
Heh?

33
00:03:37,085 --> 00:03:40,520
This person went to the same high school and university as me.

34
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:42,520
(Phone voice) Oh, right, right.

35
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:44,320
(Phone voice) I got a job at National Railways!

36
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:46,213
(Phone voice) Looking forward to working with you!

37
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:50,663
Uh, excuse me, but what is this regarding?

38
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,996
Aww, so cold!

39
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:54,640
Anyway, the real reason I'm calling.

40
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:58,780
Just between us, we've been getting nonstop alerts since this morning.

41
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:04,416
(Phone voice) Your system has been repeatedly sending large amounts of incomplete data to us.

42
00:04:04,516 --> 00:04:05,574
What!?

43
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:08,816
(Phone voice) So, take care of it ASAP, okay?

44
00:04:09,667 --> 00:04:12,750
(Phone disconnects)

45
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:13,983
(Lt. Commander) What happened?

46
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:15,908
It was Sasamin.

47
00:04:15,932 --> 00:04:17,240
Sasamin?

48
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:20,556
Sasami cutlet?

49
00:04:29,940 --> 00:04:33,501
Goodbye, leaving on time.

50
00:04:37,773 --> 00:04:40,540
Ahh...

51
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:45,340
Report!

52
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:48,500
Virus infection reports!

53
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:51,920
...None are flooding in.

54
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:55,560
In fact, there aren't any at all.

55
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:58,280
That's because Katsuragi's been going around threatening everyone.

56
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:00,840
Saying anyone who gets a virus infection faces disciplinary action.

57
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:05,100
...Can't you do something about that, CIO?

58
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:06,300
I can't!

59
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,340
He used to be a union leader -- he's a tough one.

60
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:11,740
I see.

61
00:05:12,690 --> 00:05:14,100
It's not my fault!

62
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Can't be helped.

63
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:19,160
Let's organize the information we have.

64
00:05:20,546 --> 00:05:21,595
(Lt. Commander) Right.

65
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:25,160
(Lt. Commander) The station and fare data transfer to National Railways keeps terminating abnormally.

66
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:29,600
(Lt. Commander) According to the logs, it's been happening every few seconds since around 8 AM.

67
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:35,016
At 9:35, upon request from National Railways,

68
00:05:35,149 --> 00:05:38,133
we severed the dedicated line five minutes later.

69
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:39,767
(Hiroka) Have you interviewed the relevant departments?

70
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:41,140
Yes.

71
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:42,320
Nothing wrong here.

72
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:43,780
Really, really.

73
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,400
Business as usual.

74
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:47,820
...So they say.

75
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:51,366
Smells like a cover-up...

76
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:53,600
What about the other systems?

77
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:55,200
They're sluggish overall.

78
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,580
It varies by server, but...

79
00:05:58,340 --> 00:06:00,970
Oh -- there's a deadlock.

80
00:06:00,995 --> 00:06:02,050
Where?

81
00:06:02,075 --> 00:06:04,186
In the HR system's monthly batch processing.

82
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:07,820
Looks like the spike in access triggered a latent bug.

83
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:12,780
Data distribution to the integration server has been stalled since this morning.

84
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:15,380
HR...

85
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,373
So that's why this morning...

86
00:06:18,493 --> 00:06:19,693
Urgh...

87
00:06:21,505 --> 00:06:24,101
That was a sad incident...

88
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:27,320
What about network traffic?

89
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:30,140
Still compiling the numbers, but it's significantly above normal.

90
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,180
Could it be something like packet looping?

91
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,080
I don't think so.

92
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:39,763
ARP traffic is normal.

93
00:06:40,263 --> 00:06:42,663
Then what's causing the increase?

94
00:06:43,059 --> 00:06:44,267
HTTP.

95
00:06:44,291 --> 00:06:45,499
HTTP?

96
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:50,096
Mostly traffic from internal PCs to internal servers --

97
00:06:50,230 --> 00:06:52,497
it's dozens of times higher than normal.

98
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:56,886
Some of it is leaking externally through the proxy.

99
00:06:57,020 --> 00:06:59,120
Any traces of malware!?

100
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:03,381
So far, no suspicious processes or files have been found.

101
00:07:04,019 --> 00:07:06,270
That's unsettling.

102
00:07:06,830 --> 00:07:07,960
We have no hard evidence yet.

103
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:10,500
But better safe than sorry.

104
00:07:12,180 --> 00:07:16,100
Lt. Commander, we're cutting off internet access from the business LAN.

105
00:07:16,285 --> 00:07:17,319
Yes, ma'am.

106
00:07:17,452 --> 00:07:18,460
Hosono.

107
00:07:18,485 --> 00:07:20,471
Contact Risk Management and the PR Department.

108
00:07:20,495 --> 00:07:22,480
Send a company-wide notification via groupware.

109
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:23,760
Yes.

110
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:26,308
That's all right with you, CIO?

111
00:07:26,431 --> 00:07:28,319
Will the reservation site be affected?

112
00:07:28,710 --> 00:07:29,840
Not at the moment.

113
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:31,920
However, depending on how things develop...

114
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:34,160
Not this again...

115
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:43,980
What measures do you have in place against cyberattacks?

116
00:07:44,759 --> 00:07:45,826
Well,

117
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:48,126
first, to ensure that attacks cannot reach us,

118
00:07:48,289 --> 00:07:50,193
the systems that manage train operations

119
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,727
are on an isolated network.

120
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:56,198
However, as the Stuxnet case demonstrated,

121
00:07:56,222 --> 00:07:57,926
even isolated networks

122
00:07:57,977 --> 00:08:00,440
have been identified as vulnerable to attack.

123
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,400
What if you did come under such an attack?

124
00:08:04,513 --> 00:08:05,515
Well,

125
00:08:05,540 --> 00:08:07,952
passenger safety is our top priority,

126
00:08:07,976 --> 00:08:09,313
and when something happens,

127
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:12,020
our principle is to stop all trains immediately.

128
00:08:12,507 --> 00:08:13,973
In our company's case,

129
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:15,306
when a system anomaly is detected,

130
00:08:15,573 --> 00:08:18,040
all signals immediately turn red,

131
00:08:18,306 --> 00:08:22,740
and a radio signal ordering an emergency stop is transmitted automatically.

132
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:25,800
A radio signal? What kind, specifically?

133
00:08:26,673 --> 00:08:28,173
Beep beep beep

134
00:08:28,307 --> 00:08:29,873
Beep beep beep?

135
00:08:29,898 --> 00:08:31,473
Beep beep beep

136
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:38,963
Emergency broadcast!

137
00:08:38,987 --> 00:08:40,673
It's coming from this command center!

138
00:08:40,873 --> 00:08:42,307
You've gotta be kidding.

139
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:47,340
Home signal -- proceed!

140
00:08:48,610 --> 00:08:50,760
Passing Higashi-Fukuzumi signal station.

141
00:08:52,490 --> 00:08:53,957
Wait--!

142
00:08:54,023 --> 00:08:56,923
Whaaaaaaat!?
(Emergency radio) Beep beep beep...

143
00:08:57,123 --> 00:09:02,789
Stop!

144
00:09:06,786 --> 00:09:08,390
A system anomaly detection!?

145
00:09:08,457 --> 00:09:09,623
Yes!

146
00:09:09,756 --> 00:09:11,180
Multiple signals passed at danger!

147
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:13,000
Emergency radio has also been triggered!

148
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:17,960
Main Line -- 431M, 441M, 2103D!

149
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:23,460
Reports are coming in that departure signals suddenly changed to stop aspect.

150
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:26,993
Tegarayama Station, Himeji Station, Kyotetsu-Sonobe Station.

151
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:29,260
The Sanshinki Line too!

152
00:09:29,780 --> 00:09:32,126
Don't you dare start up that train rescheduling thing!

153
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:35,060
We're not using anything that heavy!

154
00:09:35,893 --> 00:09:39,140
Whaaaat!? I didn't do anything!?

155
00:09:48,126 --> 00:09:50,026
Excuse me.

156
00:09:50,126 --> 00:09:53,260
It's been stopped for an hour now -- what's going on!?

157
00:09:53,870 --> 00:09:56,711
I'm sorry... we don't have any new information...

158
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:01,450
This is 2103D driver.

159
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:03,550
Transport Command, come in?

160
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:09,750
Transport Command, this is 2103D driver, come in?

161
00:10:11,930 --> 00:10:13,870
Transport Command, you idiots!

162
00:10:14,630 --> 00:10:15,831
(Train radio) This is Transport Command.

163
00:10:15,856 --> 00:10:17,465
(Train radio) Which driver just called us idiots? Over!

164
00:10:17,670 --> 00:10:18,670
Heh, too easy.

165
00:10:19,310 --> 00:10:23,150
This is 2103D driver. When will operations resume?

166
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:28,336
This is Transport Command to 2103D driver.

167
00:10:28,427 --> 00:10:30,059
Due to a transport management system malfunction...

168
00:10:30,227 --> 00:10:32,360
(Train radio) You told me that an hour ago!

169
00:10:32,493 --> 00:10:34,860
(Train radio) What kind of malfunction is it?

170
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:37,630
What happened, and what's the current status!?

171
00:10:38,390 --> 00:10:39,390
What's going on!?

172
00:10:39,875 --> 00:10:41,890
What the hell is going ooooon!?

173
00:10:42,895 --> 00:10:44,610
Inquiries are overwhelming us.

174
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:46,250
Like I'd know.

175
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:49,023
We're the ones who want to know the most!

176
00:10:49,357 --> 00:10:52,123
What the hell is happening? Seriously!

177
00:10:52,690 --> 00:10:55,456
What is Signal & Communications doing, huh!?

178
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:59,923
We've asked KITS and the various vendors to investigate...

179
00:10:59,957 --> 00:11:02,157
Hey! KITS's what's-his-face!

180
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:04,170
It's Uyagawa.

181
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:06,770
You know something, don't you!

182
00:11:06,970 --> 00:11:10,130
I know you do -- you were on the transport management system upgrade project!

183
00:11:10,530 --> 00:11:12,798
I only do terminal maintenance now,

184
00:11:12,822 --> 00:11:15,789
and besides, nothing has been clearly established--

185
00:11:15,889 --> 00:11:17,370
Don't tell me it's a virus!

186
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:21,889
The current situation is that we haven't found one either...

187
00:11:22,023 --> 00:11:23,290
Tch! Give me a break!

188
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:28,310
If it is a virus, I'd face disciplinary action... no way...

189
00:11:28,510 --> 00:11:30,555
My credit card payments...

190
00:11:30,579 --> 00:11:33,310
My revolving credit payments...

191
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:35,750
What exactly is the situation here?

192
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:37,370
Well, um...

193
00:11:40,820 --> 00:11:43,060
It must be a virus -- it's my fault!

194
00:11:43,540 --> 00:11:47,380
It's because of me... and if they find out, it won't just be me!

195
00:11:47,381 --> 00:11:50,273
The section chief could lose their job too.

196
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:53,540
Stop causing trouble for other people.

197
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:55,580
Oh no, what do I do!

198
00:11:55,780 --> 00:11:56,780
What do I do!

199
00:11:57,023 --> 00:11:58,723
What do I do...

200
00:13:22,257 --> 00:13:28,178
Operational Safety Code, Article 2, Item 2, Section 9: Accident Prevention

201
00:13:28,255 --> 00:13:32,043
All employees shall cooperate in the prevention of accidents,

202
00:13:32,134 --> 00:13:35,243
and shall ensure that passengers and the public are not harmed,

203
00:13:35,335 --> 00:13:39,456
making every effort to that end.
